<ArticleLayout title="Apple Watch HRV: SDNN, Readings & Stress Tracking" description="Find Apple Watch HRV in Health, understand SDNN versus RMSSD, and learn how Cortisol+ uses available readings for stress awareness." published="2026-05-23" updated="2026-09-09" image="/screenshots/health-breakdown.png">
<p><strong>Apple Watch HRV describes variation in the time between heartbeats. It does not measure cortisol.</strong> Cortisol+ uses available HRV with other wearable inputs to calculate a wellness score. Start with the measurement, its source and its timestamp before interpreting that score.</p>
<h2>Where to find Apple Watch HRV</h2>
<p>In the iPhone Health app, tap <strong>Search</strong> and look for <strong>Heart Rate Variability</strong>. Under <strong>Options</strong>, use <strong>Show All Data</strong> to inspect records and <strong>Data Sources &amp; Access</strong> to check contributing devices. This navigation follows Apple's iOS 26 documentation; labels can differ on other versions. See <a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/iphone/view-your-health-data-iphe3d379c32/26/ios/26">Apple's Health data guide</a>.</p>
<h2>What does SDNN mean?</h2>
<p>Apple's HealthKit HRV quantity is SDNN, a statistic describing variation between normal heartbeat intervals. Its unit is time, commonly displayed in milliseconds. <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/healthkit/hkquantitytypeidentifier/heartratevariabilitysdnn">Apple documents the SDNN data type</a> used by apps that read Health data.</p>
<p>SDNN and RMSSD are different calculations. A score from another app may also use a different recording window or its own interpretation. Avoid treating two numbers as interchangeable simply because both are labeled HRV. Check the method, units and recording conditions first; our <a href="/hrv/normal-range/">guide to HRV ranges and measurement methods</a> explains the comparison problem.</p>
<h2>How often does it update?</h2>
<p>Apple describes background heart-rate readings whose timing varies with activity and an optical sensor used for HRV. That is not a fixed HRV sampling schedule. A new heart-rate value does not establish that a new HRV sample exists. Check the HRV record itself. Read <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">Apple's monitoring explanation</a>.</p>
<p>If your app shows an old value, compare it with Health before assuming the watch has stopped recording. Our <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">HRV troubleshooting guide</a> covers timestamps, permissions, fit and power settings.</p>
<h2>How Cortisol+ uses HRV</h2>
<p>HRV is one input to Cortisol+'s 0–100 wellness score, alongside available sleep, heart-rate and activity data. The model starts with defaults and can blend in personal history as readings accumulate. Missing inputs and older records affect interpretation. The <a href="/how-it-works/">published methodology</a> describes the weights, baseline rules and limits.</p>
<p>The app's score is not a measured cortisol level or an observed hormone curve. We have not established its clinical accuracy against laboratory cortisol testing. It cannot tell you that a supplement worked, diagnose a hormone disorder or explain a symptom from HRV alone.</p>
<h2>A practical way to compare readings</h2>
<p>Record the source and timestamp, then note relevant context rather than assigning a cause to each change. Our <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free stress and sleep journal</a> gives you a place to record routines and observations. A pattern in that journal is a question to investigate, not proof of cause and effect.</p>
<p>For app features, explore <a href="/features/hrv-stress-monitoring/">HRV stress monitoring</a>. To compare product approaches, see our <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">Apple Watch stress app comparison</a>, which identifies the evidence behind its descriptions.</p>
